          "type": "text",
          "text": "A few of the premises here don't quite match what's actually happened, so worth untangling before the \"why.\"\n\n**Tamil Nadu \u2260 RSS liking TVK**\n\nTVK's Vijay has explicitly called the BJP and RSS \"ideological enemies\" and refused any alliance with them, \"not publicly, not even behind closed doors.\" Leaders of the AIADMK and BJP actually believed Vijay contesting alone would help the DMK, since anti-government votes would split between the AIADMK-BJP alliance, TVK, and Seeman's NTK. The BJP's actual electoral partner in TN is the **AIADMK** \u2014 the BJP announced a pre-poll alliance with AIADMK for 2026, a reversal from 2024 when it hadn't allied with AIADMK.\n\nWhat happened at the polls (April 2026) undercuts the \"RSS is happy\" theory further: TVK won 108 seats on its own, DMK fell to 59, AIADMK to 47, and Vijay became Chief Minister. Afterwards, the BJP reportedly tried to engineer a split in AIADMK to prop up TVK while keeping Congress out of power \u2014 but Vijay refused to sever ties with Rahul Gandhi. So rather than RSS gaining a friendly government, it got a CM who won't touch it and kept Congress inside his coalition.\n\n**Why the general RSS/BJP preference ordering (AIADMK > DMK/Left) still holds ideologically**\n\n- **DMK**: built on the Periyar/Self-Respect Dravidian movement \u2014 explicitly anti-Brahminical, rationalist, and hostile to Hindu-nationalist politics. That's a direct ideological collision with RSS, not just electoral rivalry.\n- **Left (CPI/CPM)**: avowedly Marxist and historically the RSS's most confrontational on-ground rival (Kerala has seen decades of CPM\u2013RSS cadre violence).\n- **AIADMK**: a populist, non-ideological Dravidian party without that anti-Hindutva baggage, which is exactly why it's been willing to ally with BJP for power (Jayalalithaa-era NDA ties, and again in 2026) \u2014 pragmatism, not affinity.\n- **TVK**: new and personality-driven, so pre-poll analysts (not RSS itself) speculated it would mainly matter as a **vote-splitter** \u2014 likely to help the BJP more than harm it by cutting into the Dravidian parties' vote bank, which is why local BJP leaders gave it a warm welcome initially. That theory of \"happy\" was about fragmentation, not alliance \u2014 and the actual result (TVK landslide, DMK collapse, AIADMK collapse, BJP still shut out) is messier than the theory predicted.\n\n**On \"RSS/Congress joining hands against the Left\"**\n\nThis is a live *accusation*, not a confirmed alignment, and it runs in both directions depending on who's talking:\n\n- In Kerala's 2026 campaign, Rahul Gandhi alleged a BJP\u2013RSS\u2013LDF nexus, claiming the BJP actually wants the Left in power because the Left poses no real national threat to it, whereas only Congress can challenge the BJP/RSS \u2014 i.e., Congress accusing the *Left* of tacit RSS convenience, the opposite of your framing.\n- Conversely, CPM's own state secretary claimed history shows cooperation between Congress and RSS-linked forces, prompting Kerala CM Pinarayi Vijayan to push back \u2014 though in that same exchange he conceded the RSS \"sees the Congress as a reliable ally,\" with no ideological common ground with the CPM itself.\n- Congress's own past rhetoric (e.g. AK Antony) has stated the opposite explicitly: Congress would at any cost stop an RSS-steered government from coming to power, seeking Left cooperation instead.\n\nSo what you're picking up on is a real, long-running pattern of **mutual \"secret nexus\" accusations** in Kerala politics (each side claims the other two are quietly colluding against it), not a documented RSS-Congress pact. Congress and the Left compete directly for the same UDF/LDF voter base, which is what generates this mudslinging \u2014 not evidence either has formally aligned with RSS.\n\nIf you want, I can dig further into either the TN post-poll alliance structure or the Kerala UDF-LDF-NDA dynamics specifically.",
